# TAIL(1)

## NAME

tail - deliver the last part of a file

## SYNOPSIS

| **tail** +_number[lbcr] [ file ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Tail* copies the named file to the standard output beginning at a designated place. If no file
is named, the standard input is used.

Copying begins at distance *+number* from the beginning, or *-number* from the end of the
input. *Number* is counted in units of lines, blocks or bytes, according to the appended option
**l**, **b** or **c**. When no units are specified, counting is by lines.

The option **r** copies lines from the end of the file in reverse order; the default for **r**
is to copy the whole buffer.

## A BLOCK IS 3072 BYTES

*Note:* **b** counts filesystem blocks, which are 3072 bytes here, where v7 counted 512-byte
PDP-11 disk blocks. So **tail -1b** delivers the last 3072 bytes and **dd bs=1b** moves the
same amount - the two commands agree, which they would not if this one had kept a number that
names nothing on this machine.

## SEE ALSO

dd(1)

## BUGS

Tails relative to the end of the file are treasured up in a buffer of 4096 bytes, and thus are
limited in length.

Various kinds of anomalous behavior may happen with character special files.
